  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will translate east across North \nCarolina today, then stall along the coast as frontal forcing \nweakens and shifts NE well away from the region. It still \nappears that the combination of the seabreeze and the stalled \nfront will support an area of enhanced low-level convergence \nfocused right along the immediate coast. Forcing will be aided \nby a shortwave moving through the area and jet entrance region \ndynamics. Meanwhile, and area of modest mid-level lapse rates \nwill overspread a moist boundary layer characterized by \ndewpoints in the 70s in the vicinity of the front. Heating of \nthis airmass should yield moderate to strong instability with \nMLCAPE of around 2000 j/kg. While low-level flow is forecast to \nbe weak, modest mid-upper level flow should support deep layer \nshear of 25-30k, favoring organized multicells. While the front \nwill be slightly removed from the strongest shear, short-term \nguidance suggests the potential for a few rotating \nupdrafts/loosely organized supercells. \n\nWhile drier air aloft will help support DCAPE of 1000j/kg+ and \nan increased potential for strong downdrafts, it may also impact\nthe sustenance of deep convection. That said, the setup appears\nsupportive of at least a few longer-lived cells capable of \ndamaging winds. If any sustained rotating updraft can develop, \nthere could also be a risk of hail. While the convective risk \nshould primarily be diurnally-driven, the front in the area plus\nplenty of elevated instability may allow a low-end thunderstorm\nrisk through the night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance is in good agreement depicting a weak \nsurface low lifting northeast along a front that should be \nstalled along the coast of the Carolinas on Monday. In fact, as \nthe low lifts through, it may pull the front back to the west \nslightly. Once again, moderate to strong instability is forecast\nto develop along the front with MLCAPE around 2000 j/kg. One \npotential difference on Monday is that deep layer shear may get \na slight boost along the track of the surface low. This may open\nthe door for a slightly improved potential for a few loosely \norganized supercells. At minimum, it should support a continued \nrisk of organized multicell clusters. With the front potentially\ngetting pulled west some, the convective risk may also expand \nwest some and not be confined to the coast only. That said, it \nstill appears that the greatest risk of strong to severe \nthunderstorms will be east of HWY 17. The Storm Prediction \nCenter has added a marginal severe weather risk for our eastern \ncounties. Once again, the primary risk looks to be damaging wind\ngusts. But, any rotating updraft will be capable of some hail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Guidance has been waffling some on whether or \nnot the airmass can fully dry out Tuesday and Wednesday as the \nSunday/Monday front may still be stalled nearby. While the \nconvective risk may not completely drop off, it looks like it \nmay be reduced for a day or two. Moisture and instability then \nbegin to rebuild across the Southeast U.S. coastline with the \npotential for another round of unsettled weather.",
